WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
We're not sure what was going on at Cartoon&nbsp;
Network's Standards and Practices department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.120
but either someone was incredibly permissive&nbsp;
or incredibly gullible, because The Powerpuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.520
Girls' writers managed to slip an unbelievable&nbsp;
amount of stuff into the show that's highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.920
inappropriate for children. Here are things&nbsp;
only adults notice in The Powerpuff Girls.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.480
If you're a connoisseur of hidden vulgarity,&nbsp;
you'll find that the dirtiest jokes in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.520
Powerpuff Girls tend to be hidden in plain sight,&nbsp;
usually on signage during establishing shots.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.800
One of the more obvious examples occurs in the&nbsp;
season two episode "Down 'n' Dirty." At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.520
Buttercup fights a monster alongside a truck&nbsp;
for a septic tank company called "Bob's Septic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.560
World." This company has the somewhat obvious,&nbsp;
yet nonetheless hilarious tagline, quote, "We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.280
Full of It!" But perhaps the most hidden and most&nbsp;
indecent joke in all of The Powerpuff Girls occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.600
in the season two episode "Something's a Ms."&nbsp;
That's where we see Ms. Bellum's house for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.640
first time. Not only is her first name Sara and&nbsp;
last name Bellum a funny pun, but according to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.320
address on her mailbox, she lives at "69 Yodelinda&nbsp;
Valley Ln." The number is a pretty famous slang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.360
and "Yodel in the valley" is a somewhat more&nbsp;
obscure, yet no less suggestive, turn of phrase.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.440
The Mayor of Townsville usually&nbsp;
seems about as chaste as they come:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.320
a cuddly and dopey little old man. But we&nbsp;
occasionally get to see the Mayor's inner psyche,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.880
and whenever we do, we find a sea of dirty&nbsp;
thoughts hanging out just beneath the surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.800
Our first hint comes in the season two&nbsp;
episode "A Very Special Blossom," when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.880
the girls are trying to raise money to buy&nbsp;
a Father's Day gift for Professor Utonium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.800
Because they don't have an actual source of&nbsp;
income, they ask the Mayor for money as back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.280
payment for all their superheroism. The Mayor&nbsp;
shuts them down with the provocative line:

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.480
"I'm no sugar daddy. Those days are over."

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.080
Things get even more obvious in the episode&nbsp;
"Something's a Ms." The supervillain Sedusa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.480
impersonates the Mayor's assistant, Miss Bellum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.320
and uses her feminine wiles to persuade&nbsp;
the Mayor to help her commit crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.880
After Sedusa is thrown in jail, the Mayor&nbsp;
continues to flirt with the real Miss Bellum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.960
until she explains that the woman flirting&nbsp;
with him earlier was a supervillain and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.000
actually her. The Mayor promptly excuses himself&nbsp;
after Ms Bellum asks where he's going and says:

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.760
"To the prison, visiting hours&nbsp;
are almost over. Toodles."

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.840
Despite all the attempts to think of&nbsp;
the Mayor as nothing but a teddy bear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.640
lines like this make it clear that he is still&nbsp;
very much sugar, spice, and everything nice.

00:02:19.356 --> 00:02:24.080
The first time the evil Rowdyruff Boys appear,&nbsp;
they act like elementary school-aged boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.160
down to their fart-based attack powers. The&nbsp;
girls eventually learn that the boys' weakness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.760
is anything feminine, so they defeat&nbsp;
the gang by kissing them on the cheeks.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.040
The second time they appear, the&nbsp;
Rowdyruff Boys have new haircuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.160
and they have grown up a little, acting&nbsp;
more like middle schoolers. The girls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.280
discover that their old enemies have&nbsp;
now lost their previous weakness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.280
Now being kissed makes them grow larger and&nbsp;
become more powerful. We don't need to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.600
the metaphor too hard for you to get this, but&nbsp;
there's symbolism in the boys growing up a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.320
and having a kiss from a girl no longer destroy&nbsp;
them but instead make them, well, larger.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.120
But the girls later discover that emasculating&nbsp;
the boys can reverse this process. By mocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.040
and belittling them, they shrink back to&nbsp;
their normal size and shrink even further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.120
until the girls can hold them in the palms of&nbsp;
their hands. Blossom even states at one point:

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.280
" Did you see that? Whenever their masculinity&nbsp;
is threatened, they shrink in size!"

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.600
Toxic masculinity does indeed&nbsp;
ruin the party yet again.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.160
Theatrical films often have difficulty&nbsp;
showing blood without getting an R rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.200
but somehow the Powerpuff Girls will regularly&nbsp;
leave villains in a bloody heap on a Y7 rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.600
show. The gals also often inflict serious&nbsp;
levels of collateral damage on Townsville,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.040
and whether or not innocent citizens&nbsp;
are hurt is rarely addressed. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.280
sometimes even implied that the Powerpuffs&nbsp;
enjoy inflicting violence on villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.680
like in the season three episode "Candy is&nbsp;
Dandy," when the girls, filled with rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.000
take beating up Mojo Jojo a little too&nbsp;
far after he steals some of their candy.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.400
But physical violence isn't the only punishment&nbsp;
the Powerpuff Girls attack with. In the season two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.440
episode "Dream Scheme," we meet the Sandman, the&nbsp;
mythological figure responsible for putting people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.760
to sleep. But since he's always working, he's&nbsp;
never able to stop and get some sleep himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.560
The Sandman has a plan to put the entire world to&nbsp;
sleep so that he can finally get some shut-eye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.400
After this works, and the Powerpuff Girls along&nbsp;
with everyone else get trapped in dream land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.280
their solution is entering his dreams and scaring&nbsp;
him so bad that, quote, "he'll never want to sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.200
again." The poor overworked creature leaps&nbsp;
out of bed and gets right back to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.840
vowing to never sleep again, a hollow smile&nbsp;
now plastered across his traumatized face.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.480
Much of The Powerpuff Girls' inappropriate&nbsp;
content occurs in small moments but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.680
season five episode "Shotgun Wedding" is raunchy&nbsp;
throughout. Professor Utonium travels into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.080
woods to study Fuzzy Lumpkins, a recurring&nbsp;
villain who is a sort of hillbilly Bigfoot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.800
a gun-wielding pink-furred monster. While tracking&nbsp;
Lumpkins, the Professor falls into some mud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.080
getting a muddy lump on his face that resembles a&nbsp;
big round nose. Lumpkins then spots Utonium and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.760
because of the mud, mistakes Utonium&nbsp;
for a female from his own species.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.160
After the Powerpuff Girls discover that Lumpkins&nbsp;
has captured their dad, intending to marry him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.720
they interrupt the wedding and beat up Fuzzy.&nbsp;
They also accidentally injure the professor a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.040
momentarily confusing him for a Lumpkin.&nbsp;
The girls apologize profusely but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.320
professor remains grateful. He&nbsp;
says rubbing his bruised butt:

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.120
"It's okay. I can think of much worse&nbsp;
things that could have happened."

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.920
The Professor cleans himself&nbsp;
off leaving behind a muddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:23.840
pile on the ground. Fuzzy, clearly upset, screams:

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.000
"Hey! Leave my wifey alone!&nbsp;
Can't you see we's in love?"

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.080
But Fuzzy just scoops up the mud, carries&nbsp;
it into his shack and completely ignores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.320
the Professor. The noises that then emanate&nbsp;
from within cannot be interpreted as anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.840
other than Lumpkins consummating&nbsp;
his marriage to a pile of wet dirt.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.720
Not all of the mature references on the The&nbsp;
Powerpuff Girls are about sex. Some are just jokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.200
about movies and music that are so adult-oriented&nbsp;
that the kids have no idea what it is.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.440
In the season two episode "Cootie Gras,"&nbsp;
there are multiple tributes to The Godfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.920
including the phrase, quote, "make you an offer&nbsp;
you can't refuse" and a recreation of the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.080
shot of the film. And in "Dream Scheme," an&nbsp;
episode in which all of the dialogue rhymes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.000
the show recreates the "Does anybody want&nbsp;
a peanut?" joke from The Princess Bride.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.360
The Powerpuff Girls writers also show&nbsp;
that they're fans of the Coen Brothers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.720
on two occasions. There's the episode&nbsp;
"Something's a Ms.," which features an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.200
extended tribute to the fireplace scene from The&nbsp;
Big Lebowski. The Mayor even quotes the line:

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.840
"Are you surprised by my&nbsp;
tears? Strong men also cry."

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.800
There's also an episode of the show called "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.920
Headsucker's Moxy," a clear&nbsp;
nod to The Hudsucker Proxy.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.000
But the most extended joke in the entire&nbsp;
series has to be the episode "Meet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.800
Beat-Alls." It's theoretically&nbsp;
an episode in which many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.560
Powerpuffs' most dangerous foes form an&nbsp;
alliance to defeat them but it's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.160
plot designed to deliver puns based&nbsp;
around the titles of Beatles songs.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.320
The creators must have had&nbsp;
fun with the show's budget.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.520
Generally speaking, most comedians realize that&nbsp;
mocking people who have been abused is immoral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.840
and not particularly funny. But for some reason,&nbsp;
cartoons have found a loophole in this rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.320
a subgroup of people who they are fine with making&nbsp;
jokes about, and that's men who were victimized in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.480
prison. Once you start to realize this trend,&nbsp;
you see that jokes about prison assaults are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.280
disturbingly common in entertainment of all&nbsp;
ages, from Marvel films to Spongebob Squarepants.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.280
But The Powerpuff Girls makes a prison&nbsp;
joke in several episodes. In "Cootie Gras,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.360
Mojo Jojo is defeated yet again by the&nbsp;
Powerpuff Girls, the episode ends with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.160
tossed into a prison cell alongside another&nbsp;
villain, a stereotypically burly tough guy.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.240
"Love is in the air! Can't you just smell it?"

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.920
There's even a callback to the&nbsp;
joke in the episode "Monkey See,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.480
Doggie Two." Mojo has been transformed into&nbsp;
a dog and finds himself in the dog pound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.400
stuck next to the similarly large and&nbsp;
imposing bulldog equivalent of his last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.160
cellmate. Most of the hidden dirty jokes&nbsp;
in The Powerpuff Girls still make us laugh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.240
But looking back at these specific jokes,&nbsp;
it's hard to find them funny or acceptable.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:46.640
Even though The Powerpuff Girls were made&nbsp;
by mixing together some chemicals in a lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.120
we get a sign in the very first episode "Mommy&nbsp;
Fearest" that the Professor has indeed told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.040
them about other forms of making babies&nbsp;
and the importance of staying protected.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.280
Professor Utonium has just met a woman he likes&nbsp;
at the supermarket and even though he is too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.040
stunned to speak to her, the girls prove that&nbsp;
they're the ultimate wingmen when they ask the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.280
woman out on The Professor's behalf. As he gets&nbsp;
ready for his date, the girls continue to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.920
him out by applying hair gel, cologne, feeding&nbsp;
him breath mints and attaching his cufflinks. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:16.640
Buttercup slips something into his jacket pocket&nbsp;
that she refers to as, quote, "some of these."

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.000
It's an extremely quick shot and we&nbsp;
don't actually see what she gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.840
but what else could that be? The show seems&nbsp;
to be heavily implying that Buttercup must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.560
have slipped the Professor some&nbsp;
protection. Just another way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.080
The Powerpuff Girls always make sure that&nbsp;
the people of Townsville are staying safe.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.800
It isn't often that an entire character is&nbsp;
created solely as a vessel for dirty jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.080
but that's what we have in the case of&nbsp;
the villain Mascumax from the episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:41.600
"Members Only". He's a hairy muscular&nbsp;
robot from outer space here to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.280
conquer the Earth and he communicates&nbsp;
exclusively through double-entendres.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.680
Mascumax first appears in a meeting of the&nbsp;
Association of World Super Men and says:

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.280
"Men of Earth, witness the coming&nbsp;
of Mascumax: breaker of men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.080
taker of worlds. Be there&nbsp;
any true men amongst thee"

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.760
"Step forth and bring thy&nbsp;
manhood against mine own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.880
so that we might see who has the&nbsp;
upper hand on the measuring stick."

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.320
There are at least five risque&nbsp;
jokes in that line alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.480
but it's totally possible that some are missed.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.040
Mascumax only sticks around for a few minutes of&nbsp;
screen time but he crams in a shocking amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.040
dirty jokes. Not long after he shows up, the&nbsp;
girls are able to defeat him by putting their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.560
powers together and transforming into a giant cat&nbsp;
made of fire known as the Furious Flaming Feline.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.480
In the season six episode "'Twas the&nbsp;
Fight Before Christmas," the girls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.160
end up at the North Pole. They learn that&nbsp;
in addition to his naughty and nice list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.400
Santa also has a permanent naughty list with&nbsp;
only four names. Three of them are references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.280
to key members of the show's creative team,&nbsp;
Craig McCracken, Lauren Faust, and Steve Fonti.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.320
But the fourth name seems to be a reference to&nbsp;
a far more naughty and far more real evildoer.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.880
The fourth name on Santa's Permanent Naughty&nbsp;
Plaque is Adolph Schicklgruber, a name which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.600
thought to be the birth name of Adolph Hitler. If&nbsp;
Santa does indeed have a Permanent Naughty Plaque,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.200
it would certainly make sense for this real&nbsp;
life villain's name to be on it. But there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.000
no actual evidence that Hitler ever had the last&nbsp;
name Schicklgruber. Schicklgruber was the surname&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.760
Hitler's father was given at birth, but&nbsp;
he changed it to "Hitler" over a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.920
before Adolph was born. It's possible that the&nbsp;
writers of The Powerpuff Girls believed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.320
urban legend about the birth name, but it's&nbsp;
far more likely that this bit of misdirection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.160
is the only way the writers could slide&nbsp;
a real world bad guy into a kids show.

